 r"I am writing to apply for the 2027 Graduate Analyst programme at Zopa. I completed an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London in September 2026, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have worked on turning open-ended questions into analyses that can be tested, building predictive models, and explaining to non-technical colleagues what the results do and do not support. I am particularly interested in this role because the programme trains across exactly the areas I have been working in: SQL and Python, applications of large language models, data science, and the economics behind product decisions.",

 r"What attracts me most to Zopa is that analysts are described as shaping the business rather than reporting on it, with ownership from the start. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved. The rotational structure appeals to me for the same reason my background is broad: I have moved between biology, healthcare research, consulting and e-commerce, and each move has made me quicker at learning a new domain well enough to ask useful questions in it.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I mine merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python to identify the growth and operations drivers behind the UK marketplace, and I build KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations, product and commercial teams use to set targets and track initiatives. I also built an AI agent using Claude Code that automates repetitive steps of the analytics workflow, now adopted by my own team and by partner teams. That was a tool built for colleagues rather than a model built for a paper, and it taught me that the deliverable is adoption, not the code.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern applying large language models to a real business problem for a pharmaceutical client. I built a Python application calling six LLM APIs with retrieval and multi-step agent workflows, producing and scoring more than 2,400 responses, and designed a 246-question benchmark and six-dimension rubric which the client adopted as their acceptance standard. I then ran blinded paired A/B tests to measure whether a reviewed knowledge base improved answer quality, and reported which differences the evidence supported and which it did not. Your Risk Lab's use of language models for problems like financial crime and customer experience is the closest thing I have seen to that work outside healthcare.",

 r"My MSc dissertation is where my predictive modelling is strongest. I built baseline risk models on 458,840 UK Biobank participants, comparing logistic regression against three tree ensembles including gradient boosting, with attention to calibration rather than discrimination alone, then applied doubly robust causal forests to estimate the effect of lifestyle changes, with sensitivity analyses for unmeasured confounding and external validation on a second cohort. It is delivered as a deployed application so that others can run scenarios without touching the code.",

 r"At Ipsos Strategy3 I worked on healthcare and consumer engagements, structuring data from 80 or more expert interviews into quantitative variables, building market-sizing and forecasting models, and turning the analysis into dashboards and recommendations for stakeholders. That is where I learned to present a finding to an audience who wants the implication rather than the method.",

 r"I should be clear that my background is not in financial services. I have not worked on lending, credit risk or product economics, and I would be learning the commercial context from the start. What I would bring instead is strong SQL and Python, genuine experience building and validating predictive models, hands-on work with large language models, and the habit of checking whether what I built actually worked before recommending it. I have the right to work in the UK until January 2029 without sponsorship. I am based in London and can start in February or September 2027.",

 r"Thank you for considering my application. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how my experience in analytics, predictive modelling and applied AI could contribute to Zopa's Analytics teams.",
